#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tools/globals-census -- plan_globals.md step G0: every symbol with STATIC
STORAGE DURATION on the server (or client) link line, and whether it is state.

    tools/globals-census census                      # the TSV, one row per symbol
    tools/globals-census summary                     # the counts the plan quotes
    tools/globals-census reconcile                   # objects vs the linked .so
    tools/globals-census gate                        # ledger membership, both ways
    tools/globals-census --self-test

=============================================================================
WHAT IT MEASURES
=============================================================================

`plan.md` and `plan_shim.md` say the port's C++ holds no static state, and
until this tool nothing measured that.  `tools/statics-audit` once did, from
the SOURCE TEXT, and that is exactly why a global moved inside an inline
accessor passed it: `PhysicsShell()::s_Shell` is 266,392 bytes of state that
no source grep for `^static` will ever find.

So STATE HERE IS A SECTION, NOT A KEYWORD.  A symbol is state when it is an
`OBJECT`, `TLS` or `GNU_UNIQUE` symbol landing in `.bss*`, `.data*`, `.tbss*`
or `.tdata*` of an object the build links.  A function-local static is state.
A class static is state.  A static inside an inline accessor in a header is
state.  `.rodata*` and `.data.rel.ro*` are counted apart and are NOT state --
the first is constants, the second vtables and typeinfo the loader relocates
once.

=============================================================================
THE FOUR METHOD RULES
=============================================================================

 1. OBJECTS COME FROM THE LINK EDGE, never from the objs directory.  That
    directory keeps the objects of translation units the build has evicted --
    on 2026-09-19 it held 190 server objects for a graph that links 144, and
    counts taken over it were wrong by up to 13x (memory:
    objs-directory-is-not-the-link-line).  This tool reads the .so's link
    edge in the CMake build's build.ninja (tools/cmakegraph.py) and nothing
    else, and refuses a compile_commands.json that disagrees with the
    CMakeLists source lists.

 2. SOURCE TEXT IS NEVER THE INSTRUMENT.  `BEGIN_DATADESC`,
    `IMPLEMENT_SERVERCLASS`, `CON_COMMAND` and the Steam SDK accessors all
    expand to a `static` the text does not contain.  `readelf -sW` decides;
    `nm -l`'s DWARF line and the source line it points at are DISPLAY, carried
    so a human can rule on a row.

 3. MATCH THE SECTION EXACTLY.  `kind ~ /bss|data/` is a substring match and
    also matches `.rodata` and `.data.rel.ro`.  Re-running this census on
    2026-09-21 with that filter returned 11,820 rows from a 1,017-row mutable
    population -- a number four times the whole symbol table's plausible
    answer, which is the tell.  `kind` is one of a CLOSED set of tokens here
    and MUTABLE_KINDS is a set, never a regex.  --self-test pins it.

 4. A CENSUS THAT CANNOT MEASURE MUST REFUSE.  Exit 2, never a quiet answer:
    a graph naming an object that is not on disk, an object older than the
    source the graph compiles it from, or an empty link edge.  An unbuilt tree
    reading as "0 statics, all clear" is the failure this whole file guards
    (memory: a-check-log-has-no-provenance, buildcpptests-exits-zero-without-libs).

=============================================================================
WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO
=============================================================================

  - It does not rule.  A disposition comes from docs/globals-ledger.tsv, which
    a human wrote; this tool joins symbols to rows and says which side is
    missing one.  It never invents a disposition, and `category` and `scope`
    below are HINTS for whoever rules, not verdicts.
  - It does not read the objs directory, `ls`, or `ld`'s output.  `ld` does not
    reliably name the objects it consumed (memory: ld-stops-naming-objects), so
    linker output is a floor and not a census.
  - It does not count a link-level symbol twice.  LOCAL symbols are counted
    once PER OBJECT (two translation units' `static int s_n;` are two distinct
    pieces of state); UNIQUE, WEAK and GLOBAL once per NAME (one piece of state
    the linker folds).
  - It does not answer about a tree it cannot identify.  Every report is
    stamped with the git tip and the dirty paths.

=============================================================================
THE COUNTING RULE, WRITTEN OUT, BECAUSE THE THREE NUMBERS DIFFER
=============================================================================

    population     every OBJECT/TLS/GNU_UNIQUE symbol in any of the six
                   section kinds, one row per (object, symbol).   ~12,300
    object level   population, restricted to MUTABLE_KINDS, minus guard
                   variables and minus the four vec2_/vec4_ header constants
                   (one runtime-constructed copy per TU under -O0).
    link level     object level, with LOCAL folded per object and everything
                   else folded per name.  THIS is the number the plan tracks
                   and the ratchet caps.

The vec exclusion is not cosmetic: at -O0 the four of them account for 406 of
the 958 rows that would otherwise be object level on this tree and all four vanish under -O2, so a
census that kept them would report the debug build's optimisation level rather
than the port's state.
"""

